# A block that uses itself, drawing a fractal: a spiral that uses itself with a smaller number, then a
# branching tree that uses itself twice with half its number, which draws the same shape at every
# size. The turtle draws on the flat rock beside the waterfall in the gorge.
lesson coding-a-block-that-uses-itself v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A block that uses itself"
  goal "Follow a block of your own that uses itself with a smaller number, and count what it draws."

  look {
    scene 36x23 {
      turtle t cols=5 rows=6 col=3 row=6 face=up moves=["define tree n", "  forward n", "  if n is more than 1", "    turn left", "    tree n / 2", "    turn around", "    tree n / 2", "    turn left", "  back n", "tree 4"] at=canvas(0, 0)
      program p code=["define tree n", "  forward n", "  if n is more than 1", "    turn left", "    tree n / 2", "    turn around", "    tree n / 2", "    turn left", "  back n", "tree 4"] text=true right-of=t gap=1
      kingfisher kf flying=0 below=t gap=1
    }
    say """
      The block tree draws a trunk, and then, on lines 5 and 7, uses itself twice with half the
      number, once to the left and once to the right. Each smaller tree does the same, until n is 1
      and the if on line 3 says no. So tree 4 draws a trunk of 4, two branches of 2 and four twigs of
      1: the same shape at every size, which is called a fractal.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.self-spiral s=4 easy-s=3 hard-s=5
    level easy {
      worked coding.self-spiral s=4
    }
    show coding.self-calls k=1 q=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.self-picture k=1 easy-k=0
    show coding.self-moves k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.self-build s=4 easy-s=3 hard-s=5
    show coding.self-calls k=2 q=1 easy-k=1 easy-q=0
    show coding.self-moves k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
    show coding.self-spiral s=5 hard-s=6
    level hard {
      show coding.self-calls k=1 q=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.self-stop k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.self-grow k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A block can use itself with a smaller number, and an if inside it says when to stop."
  }

  grown-ups """
    A block that uses itself is called recursion. The two things to see are that each use has its
    own smaller number, and that an if stops it when the number is small enough; without the if it
    would never finish. The common mistake is to think the block runs once, or that the number goes
    back to where it started after the first use. In the child's working, look for the sizes written
    in a column, 4, then 2 and 2, then 1, 1, 1 and 1, rather than the lines of the program. If the
    child is stuck, act it out: the child is tree 4 and draws a trunk, then asks two helpers to be
    tree 2, who each ask two more. Off the page, look for fractals in a fern leaf, a cauliflower or a
    river's branches on a map. The two-star question asks what stops the block; the three-star
    question follows the pattern 1, 3, 7, 15 past a big number.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the spirals have three to five sides, one is worked in the teacher's pen, the trees are tree 2 and tree 4, the picture to read is tree 2, and the pattern goes past 20 lines."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the spirals have five or six sides, the trees go up to tree 8, the questions ask how often the line inside the if runs, a third tree question is added, the what-stops-it question changes the if rather than taking it out, and the pattern goes past 100 lines."
  }
}
